  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Monday...\n\nSatellite imagery shows mostly clear skies within a zonal flow\naloft, but we are starting to see an increase in mid/high clouds\nfrom the west. Upstream radar is suggestive of a band of light \nshowers moving into south central MT. Forecast soundings show this\nto be tied to very elevated moisture/instability (based near \n500mb). Will hold off on any mention of showers late this \nafternoon/tonight and will continue to monitor. Surface analysis \nshows a 1020mb high over AB, which will sag over eastern MT \ntonight in response to a shortwave currently over southern SK. \nThis will bring a shift from N to NE-E winds tonight, setting up a\n\"cooler\" day with easterly winds tomorrow. Regarding air quality,\nmany reporting stations are showing moderate air quality. The \nbackdooring winds should improve near surface smoke tonight, but \nthe winds aloft will continue to support hazy skies resulting from\nsmoke transport from the numerous wildfires in WA/OR.\n\nPer the monsoonal flow, air mass moistens considerably tomorrow \nw/ pwats rising back to anomalously high values (1.2-1.4\").\nForcing seems lacking tomorrow so the greatest potential for wet\nthunderstorms will be over southern mountains/foothills, eking\ninto southeast MT tomorrow night w/ a southeasterly LLJ. Models\nare consistently showing a distinct shortwave emerging from the\ngreat basin Wednesday, which should yield a greater chance of\nthunderstorms over most of the region (though still highest pops\nin west and south portions of the cwa). Wet thunderstorm potential\ncontinues Thursday on the back side of this wave. All told, any\nthunderstorms the next 3 days will have the potential to produce\nvery heavy rainfall and localized wet microbursts. Given the\nexpectation of greatest coverage of storms over our south, burn\nscars such as Robertson Draw, East Side and Elk will need to be \nwatched for a risk of flash flooding. Temperatures will be \nseasonable through the period.\n\nExpect hotter & drier conditions Friday thru Monday, with building\nconfidence based on ensembles/cluster analyses. Monsoon moisture\nwill be reduced and heights will build well into the 590s. There \nremains a slight chance (10-20%) of thunderstorms on these days, \nbut they will be high-based and drier. With 700mb temperatures\nrising to +16C to +18C we will likely see triple digits, perhaps\nover multiple days especially for notoriously hotter spots in the\neast (e.g. Miles City and Broadus). Mixed winds will be breezy\nduring the daytimes, and there may also be a pre-frontal day of\nconcern if the ridge flattens into early next week. All of these\ndetails are highly uncertain now, but overall expect fire danger\nto increase over this period of time.\n\nAs a side note, the Billings airport has not (since 1934) reached\n100F on July 25th...the only such day in July, and in fact the\nonly day between June 23 and August 19. This might be the year\nthe climate oddity finally ends. Current forecast for Saturday is\n101F. The fly in the ointment, however, could be wildfire smoke \nas winds aloft do support smoke transport from fires in WA/OR/CA. \nStay tuned.\n\nJKL",
